WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:10.040
Welcome to Project Pannonia, a collaboration
involving 11 History YouTubers all doing videos

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.260
on various events in the History of the Pannonian
basin.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:17.850
From the Roman era all the way to Hungary's
involvement in WW2.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:21.820
And what better way to start off this colab
then with the people that created the province

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:25.620
that gave these grasslands the name of Pannonia,
the Romans.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:29.760
Prior to the Roman conquest of Pannonia, we
don’t really know much about the area or

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.360
the people living in it.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:36.050
However looking through archeology and “very”
brief mentions of the area in Latin and Greek

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:39.550
sources we can gather small clues as to what
happened.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:44.380
At the beginning of the 4th century BCE there
was a big Celtic invasion of Pannonia which

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:47.149
coincided with the Celtic invasion of Italy.

00:00:47.149 --> 00:00:51.670
This invasion was mostly centered around going
down the Danube and Drava rivers.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:56.670
The people that inhabited the area before
the Celts were mostly the illyrian tribe of

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:57.670
Pannonii.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:01.469
When they got pushed out by the Celts this
tribe moved south settling around the Sava

00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:02.469
river.

00:01:02.469 --> 00:01:07.490
However despite this resettlement in the 3rd
century BCE the Pannonii eventually succumbed

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:10.870
to a celtic invasion by a tribe called Scordisci.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:15.600
During this time eastern Panonnia and Transilvania
was mostly controlled by the Dacians a subgroup

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.640
of Threcians but that didn’t stop the Celts
migrating there as well.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:24.230
For the next couple of centuries the Pannonian
area underwent large celticization with the

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:30.500
Dacian lands having mixed Dacian/Celtic culture
and the Scordisci controlled Pannonii having

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:33.540
a mixed Illirian/Celitc culture.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:37.910
However even though the entire area was under
the celtic influence, this is not to say it

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:39.410
was all unified.

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:43.470
There where many bickering celtic tribes that
didn’t seem to be the best of friends.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:48.250
Some of the most notable ones where the Boii
tribal alliance who were driven out of northern

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:54.200
Italy by the Romans in 193 and settled in
Northern Pannonia, the Taurisci inhabiting

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:59.810
western Pannonia, the Scordisci around the
Sava river, and of course the celtic Dacians

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:02.200
in Eastern Pannonia and Transilvania.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:08.600
In around 88 to 81 BCE the Scordisci which
were struggling to keep control over the Pannonii

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:14.460
for several decades now where decisively defeated
by a Roman general Lucius Cornelius Scipio

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:18.550
Asiaticus, no not that one, his grade grandson,
yeah that one.

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:19.550
How unimaginative where the romans, It is
a freaking 4 part name and yet there still

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:20.550
manages to be two of them with the exact same
name, But I digress.

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:24.100
After this defeat the Scordisci disappear
being replaced by their captives the Pannonii.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:29.530
Also starting during this time until around
45 BCE the Thracian king Burebista solidified

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:33.640
the dacian lands and conquered all of pannonia
east of the Danube.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:37.940
This Dacian pressure from the East, newly
liberated Illirian pressure from the South

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:42.450
and Roman pressure from the west destroyed
the larger celtic political entities that

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:47.860
existed up until now and the celtic control
of Pannonia became very fragmented.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:52.130
When exactly the basin became part of the
Roman empire is very much debated, but most

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:56.960
likely due to the now fragmented political
nature of the area it was a gradual process

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:01.150
done under the reign of Agustus from around
35 to 9 BCE.

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:05.230
It was also during this time that the Dacian
control of Eastern pannonia slowly withered

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:09.770
away as the kingdom struggled to keep together
after the death of Burebista.

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:14.900
In 11 BCE the frontier of the Roman Empire
was officially set all the way to the Danube

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:20.700
river and then 2 years later this new land
was incorporated to the provinc of Illiricum.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:25.840
However certain areas of this frontier weren’t
very loyal to the Empire and the Romans didn’t

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:28.630
really have any major control in the countryside.

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:32.590
This along with the local resentment against
the Romans resulted in the Great Illirian

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:34.760
revolt in 6 CE.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:38.420
However this mainly involved just the Illirians
in southern Pannonia as the Celts further

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:41.540
north seemed to have been content with the
Roman rule.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:46.670
When the revolt was finally subdued by Tiberius
and Germanicus three years later, illiricum

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:50.430
was deemed to be too large to govern effectively
and so it was divided up.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:51.430
One of these newly created provincies was
the province of Pannonia.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:56.050
It was also in the early 1st century that
the Sarmantian tribe of Iazyges migrated in

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:58.200
to the area east of the Danube.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:02.980
Whether the Romans invited them to come here
and act as a buffer between them and the Dacians

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:07.970
or they migrated on their own using the fracturing
of the Dacian kingdom to their benefit, is

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:09.090
still debated.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:14.760
What’s also debated is the origin of the
name Pannonia but in my opinion it most likely

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:20.160
comes from an Illirian word which meant swampy
land or wetland, which makes since as the

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:25.190
pannonian basin is just a giant tributary
for the Danubian river and Illirians were

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:37.720
the original inhabitants of this area.

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:41.950
Now Roman Pannonia didn’t stretch across
the entire pannonian basin, it encompassed

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:45.750
only the areas of the basin West and South
of the Danube.

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:49.669
However with that said this Roman province
name started to be used to refer to the entire

00:04:49.669 --> 00:04:54.260
geographical location of the basin in the
medieval period, hence why we call this entire

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:56.889
area the Pannonian Basin or plain today.

00:04:56.889 --> 00:05:01.100
When it came to administrative matters Pannonia
actually underwent several changes throughout

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:02.410
the Roman period.

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:06.290
First being the province of Pannonia then
dividing in to two Pannonias then in to four

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:08.630
then back again in to one, etc.

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:12.330
However I am going to be mostly skipping over
these divisions referring to Pannonia as a

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:13.330
whole.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:17.940
So from the year 9 at the end of the Illirian
revolt Pannonia was finally firmly under Roman

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:21.270
control and it will stay like this until the
5th century.

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:27.300
To put this in to perspective, Romans controlled
Pannonia for longer than USA has been a country

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:29.500
plus about 200 years more.

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:34.290
With this much time of Roman rule in the area
it is undeniable that they left their mark

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:35.660
on these lands.

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:39.580
The first major change that came with the
Romans was the creation of cities.

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:44.310
Yes there where population establishments
like celtic oppidums before the Roman arrival

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:49.919
but the majority of these were more of a “fortified
village” rather than an actual city.

00:05:49.919 --> 00:05:55.610
However as Roman colonists arrived combined
with the slow latinization of the native population,

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:58.040
Pannonia gradually became more urbanized.

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:02.000
This urbanization was also helped in part
by the creation of the Roman road network

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.900
which the Romans build mainly for an ease
of supply to the legions stationed in Pannonia.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:11.450
Along with building up these major roads and
cities, 3 of which are capital cities today

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:15.180
the Romans also did some engineering work
on the Danube.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:19.100
This is because some parts of the Danube back
then where very treacherous for boats and

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:22.220
sailing on it could be very costly for supply
fleets.

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:27.090
But the Romans remedied this making the river
much more safer to sail on and also established

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:30.710
the first known Danubian fleet station in
Carnuntum.

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:35.300
This fleet was used to not only supply the
cities along the river but also to protect

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:38.270
the border, as the Danube was the border.

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:43.040
The Romans also brought wine making to the
region, established numerous bath houses,

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:46.590
and over all brought drastic economic development
to the area.

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:51.090
When it came to legions stationed in Pannonia
the amount and type of legions changed over

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:56.310
time but from the Hadrian era all the way
to late antiquity there were roughly 6 main

00:06:56.310 --> 00:06:57.310
ones.

00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:00.210
The 10th Legion Gemina stationed in Vindobona
todays Vienna

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:04.750
The 14th Legion Gemina stationed in Carnuntum
todays Petronell Carnuntum

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:09.320
The 1st Legion Adiutrix stationed in Brigetio
todays Komarom

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:13.470
The 2sc Legion Adiutrix stationed in Aquincum
today’s Budapest

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:17.160
The 4th Legion Flavia stationed in Singidunum
today’s Belgrade

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:21.669
And lastly
The 7th Legion Claudia stationed in Viminacium

00:07:21.669 --> 00:07:27.040
As you may have noticed the area between Budapest
and Belgrade had a very large empty border

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:29.300
without any Legione protecting it.

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:33.729
This was mainly due to the very ineffective
way that legiones where assigned to various

00:07:33.729 --> 00:07:38.570
Roman provinces but since its very boring
to talk about bureaucracy we can just conclude

00:07:38.570 --> 00:07:40.600
that the Romans made a mistake.

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:45.850
Due to this mistake however the 2sc and 4th
legione had to protect a much larger border

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:49.230
then for example the 10th, 14th and 1st legiones.

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:53.600
This resulted in the fact that the Second
legione was by far the most active legion

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:58.819
in Pannonia having to go on many expeditionary
campaigns within and outside of the Roman

00:07:58.819 --> 00:08:01.150
borders, to secure peace in the regione.

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:05.590
While this was happening the 4th legion was
always transferred to Budapest to protect

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:07.110
from any rear attacks.

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:11.150
This high activity of the Second legion in
Pannonia along with that fact that it was

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:15.580
stationed in today's Budapest where some of
the reasons why we decided to go with the

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:20.010
symbol of the second legion the pegasus for
our Project Pannonia colab.

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:24.550
The relative peace that was had by the Romans
in Pannonia was broken in 85.

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:28.840
This was because at this point the Dacian
kingdom which was fragment ever since the

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:34.079
death of Burebista finally united again and
started to hold raiding expeditions across

00:08:34.079 --> 00:08:35.079
the Danube.

00:08:35.079 --> 00:08:39.380
Most of these weren’t held in Pannonia but
in Moesia more south, however Pannonian legions

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:41.719
did play a vital role in this conflict.

00:08:41.719 --> 00:08:46.230
The Romans eventually brokered a peace with
the Dacians but Romans being Romans knowing

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:51.980
this won't last started to built up many auxiliary
fortifications along the Danube, getting ready

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:55.130
for the eventual second war with the Dacians.

00:08:55.130 --> 00:09:01.400
This war came in 101 ending with a Roman victory
in 102 but peace didn’t last as the third

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:07.760
Dacian war started in 105 with the Romans
again winning in 106 but this time they made

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:10.300
sure there won't be a 4th Dacian war.

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:15.730
They subdued the entire area and incorporated
in to the Roman Empire as a new province.

00:09:15.730 --> 00:09:20.010
The Iazyges during this time where doing a
bit of flip flopping with their alliances

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:24.690
between the Dacians and Romans, however in
the end these wars resulted in a much more

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:36.170
direct control by the Romans over them, becoming
a client state between 117 and 123.

00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:42.750
From there for about 50 plus years Pannonians
lived in relative peace, making wine, bathing,

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:46.170
socializing, trading, and whatever else the
Romans do.

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:52.100
However this all came to an end during the
outbreak of the Marcomannic Wars in 162.

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:56.320
These wars were the first signs of a large
scale migrations happening outside of the

00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:01.690
Roman borders and many tribes that were already
living near the Roman border had nowhere to

00:10:01.690 --> 00:10:03.990
go but across that border.

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:09.030
And so Panonnia got invaded first by Germanic
tribes from north and then by the Iazyges

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:10.420
from the east.

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:14.959
However these invasions managed to be repelled
by the Romans, after which the Romans launched

00:10:14.959 --> 00:10:20.100
a counterattack against the Iazyges all while
yet another Germanic army crossed from the

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:25.680
north devastating Vienna and marching all
the way south to northern Italy before being

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:26.680
defeated.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:30.310
After this the Romans undertook a large scale
offencive North which proved successful and

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:35.420
the Roman Emperor Marcu Aurelius even considered
creating buffer provinces north of Pannonia

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:40.060
to protect from any other future attacks,
but this did not happen as he died in 180

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:44.600
and his successor Commodus didn’t follow
through on these victories.

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:49.490
Commodus however did set out on another campaign
against Iazyges scoring a quick victory.

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:54.051
At this point it is safe to say that the Iazyges
where firmly under Roman control, however

00:10:54.051 --> 00:10:56.750
what kind of control this was is unknown.

00:10:56.750 --> 00:11:00.980
This combined with frequent small rebellions
and wars by the Iazyges throughout the next

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:05.769
century or so means that even though they
surely where controlled by the Romans the

00:11:05.769 --> 00:11:12.310
area east of the Danube was never fully incorporated
in to the Empire as for example Dacia was.

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:15.870
However one argument against this could be
the fact that the construction of the Devil’s

00:11:15.870 --> 00:11:20.280
Dyke started around the later half of the
second century probably used as part of the

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:21.620
Roman Limes.

00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:26.100
Hence we can conclude the Romans did consider
this area as part of their Empire, even with

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:28.920
all the frequent Iazyges rebeliones.

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:33.490
After the Marcomannic wars the Pannonian area
became settled by a lot of Germanic people

00:11:33.490 --> 00:11:37.870
that where either allowed to settle here or
used the confusion of the wars to cross the

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:41.390
border or even where the remnants of the defeated
Germanic armies.

00:11:41.390 --> 00:11:42.390
These germanic settlers probably numbered
as much as the latin people living in it Pannonia

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:43.390
at this point.

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:47.410
However as it often does, during the next
couple of decades of “roughly” peace these

00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:52.149
germanic people got slowly latinized just
as the Celts had before them.

00:11:52.149 --> 00:11:56.390
The next major step in Roman Pannonian history
came during the Christianization of the Roman

00:11:56.390 --> 00:11:57.390
Empire.

00:11:57.390 --> 00:12:02.540
It seems that outside of Sirmium most of Pannonia
was very much starchly pagan until Constantine

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:03.800
came to power.

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:08.760
After which almost comically quickly the Pannonian
people, especially the higher class converted

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:14.360
to christianity, with pagan burials being
found increasingly rarely after early 4th

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:15.360
century.

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:19.579
It was also during Constantine's era that
the Vandals showed up on the fringes of the

00:12:19.579 --> 00:12:22.700
Pannonian border being pushed their by the
Goths.

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:26.760
Constantine allowed the Vandals to settle
on the right bank of the Danube most likely

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:28.890
to control the troublesome Iazyges.

00:12:28.890 --> 00:12:33.180
It was also at this point that the Devils
Dyke reached its highest extend meaning that

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:38.140
Romans were probably hoping to keep control
of the entire Pannonian plain for a while.

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:43.060
However all things must come to an end, and
the roughly 440 year long Roman control of

00:12:43.060 --> 00:12:44.960
Pannonia was no different.

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:50.800
By the late Roman Period the border regions
had a constant activity of intertribal wars,

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:52.959
migrations, alliances, etc.

00:12:52.959 --> 00:12:56.971
This combined with wavering internal Roman
politics meant that it was clear the Roman

00:12:56.971 --> 00:12:59.460
hold on Pannonia wasn’t going to last.

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:04.190
There were several tribes like the Vandals,
Goths, etc. that attacked Pannonia in the

00:13:04.190 --> 00:13:09.170
late 4th and early 5th century but the Romans
still managed to keep hold of the area until

00:13:09.170 --> 00:13:10.519
the arrival of the Huns.

00:13:10.519 --> 00:13:16.040
It is known that the Huns controlled much
of Pannonia by 437 but how and when exactly

00:13:16.040 --> 00:13:20.500
did they conquered it is unknown but most
likely starting in around 420.

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:25.700
They probably did it the same way the Romans
conquered Pannonia well over 400 years earlier,

00:13:25.700 --> 00:13:31.130
it was a gradual taking of control by the
Huns with interspersed Roman expeditions trying

00:13:31.130 --> 00:13:33.320
to hold on to whatever they could.

00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:37.610
It is also interesting to learn that the area
of specially southern Pannonia and Moesia

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:43.370
was given back and forth between the Western
and Eastern Roman Empires as both sides clearly

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:45.430
didn’t want to deal with the Hunnic raids.

00:13:45.430 --> 00:13:51.470
The official Roman control of Pannonia is
dated to be until 447 when the Eastern Roman

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:56.500
empire finally gave up the claims to the Pannonian
lands but as we discussed, the Romans didn’t

00:13:56.500 --> 00:14:01.460
really control the area for decades at this
point.

00:14:01.460 --> 00:14:05.750
That is where I will conclude my video about
Roman pannonia because it’s time for Epimetheus

00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:08.550
to take the torch and talk about the Huns.

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:13.310
Also check out the last video in the Project
Pannonia colab done by Historigraph who’s

00:14:13.310 --> 00:14:15.790
talking about Hungarian involvement in WW2.

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:20.189
If you liked my videos please consider subscribing
and stick around for history.

